{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba include/strata/core/weights.hpp | sed -n '1,205p'; rg -n '\"'^(bool|void|uint64_t|const |[A-Za-z]+::)|check_|geometry|native_experts|dense.bin|experts.bin|pack'\"' src/core/weights.cpp | head -130; nl -ba include/strata/core/layout.hpp | sed -n '1,155p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t﻿// include/strata/core/weights.hpp - the dense weights, loaded into VRAM in ENGINE form.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// The pack is the ARTIFACT's business; this is the ENGINE's.  The differences are deliberate, and they run in\n     4\t// BOTH directions:\n     5\t//\n     6\t//   * SHRINKING.  A tensor whose source type is BF16 is stored in the pack promoted to 32 bits, because that is\n     7\t//     lossless and it keeps the canonical decoder simple, but a running engine re-rounds it to 16 - which costs\n     8\t//     nothing at all, because the value CAME from 16 bits.  Per `tools/pack_budget.py` that saving is the\n     9\t//     difference between the dense weights fitting in VRAM beside the expert cache and not fitting at all.\n    10\t//\n    11\t//   * GROWING.  90 of the 303 quantized tensors carry fp16 SCALES in the pack (all 58 Q2_0 expert tensors, plus\n    12\t//     Q4_0, Q5_0, Q8_0 and IQ4_NL) because that is what the source block holds.  Every kernel here takes\n    13\t//     `const float* scales`, and fp16 is a subset of f32, so this loader WIDENS them - exactly, changing no\n    14\t//     value - for 11,407,360 B.  See `docs/pack-format.md` Â§2.4; assuming 4 bytes per scale instead of reading\n    15\t//     `scales_fp16` is what made the plane arithmetic disagree with the pack for a whole round.\n    16\t//\n    17\t//     pack      5.305 GiB dense + 0.444 GiB embd\n    18\t//     engine    4.067 GiB dense + 0.444 GiB embd + 10.88 MiB widened scales = 4.522 GiB\n    19\t//\n    20\t// WHAT THE LOADER DOES NOT DO: it does not know what any tensor MEANS.  It reads a flat index\n    21\t// (`<pack>/index.txt`, written by `tools/pack_index.py`), copies each tensor into one arena at a recorded\n    22\t// offset - as a list of PLANE SEGMENTS, because a scale plane can change width and the rest cannot - and hands\n    23\t// back a name -> WeightRef map.  Geometry, forms and semantics belong to the caller.\n    24\t//\n    25\t// WHY AN INDEX FILE AND NOT A JSON PARSER: a JSON parser in the engine would be a new, unaudited component\n    26\t// whose failure mode is a wrong byte offset - which decodes to a plausible weight and produces plausible\n    27\t// logits.  Python wrote the manifest and already parses it correctly.\n    28\t#pragma once\n    29\t\n    30\t#include <cstdint>\n    31\t#include <map>\n    32\t#include <set>\n    33\t#include <string>\n    34\t#include <vector>\n    35\t\n    36\tnamespace strata::core {\n    37\t\n    38\t/// What the loader had to DO for a tensor, decided by `tools/pack_index.py` and written into the index\n    39\t/// rather than inferred here.  The four cases are distinguishable in the manifest only by combining\n    40\t/// `source_type` with `values_fp16`, and getting that combination wrong yields a plausible tensor.\n    41\tenum class WeightKind : int {\n    42\t    Verbatim = 0,   ///< quantized planes, or F16 already stored as 2 bytes: copy straight through\n    43\t    Bf16InF32 = 1,  ///< the pack holds the bf16 value promoted to f32: take the HIGH 16 BITS (exact)\n    44\t    F32 = 2,        ///< copy 4 bytes per element\n    45\t    F16InF32 = 3,   ///< an f16 value promoted to f32: a real f32->f16 conversion, not a truncation\n    46\t};\n    47\t\n    48\tstruct WeightRef {\n    49\t    const void* data = nullptr;   ///< device pointer inside the arena\n    50\t    uint64_t bytes = 0;\n    51\t    int64_t ne0 = 0;              ///< the manifest's own shape, ne0 the CONTIGUOUS axis\n    52\t    int64_t ne1 = 0;\n    53\t    int64_t elements = 0;\n    54\t    WeightKind kind = WeightKind::Verbatim;\n    55\t    /// The canonical-form attributes, valid only for a quantized VERBATIM tensor (`code_bits != 0`).\n    56\t    int code_bits = 0;\n    57\t    int code_bias = 0;\n    58\t    int group_elems = 0;\n    59\t    bool codebook_iq4nl = false;\n    60\t    bool has_offset = false;\n    61\t\n    62\t    /// THE THREE PLANE SIZES, IN ENGINE FORM, in the order they sit inside `data`.  Recorded by the loader\n    63\t    /// from what the index TOLD it rather than re-derived by each caller: the one time this layout was\n    64\t    /// re-derived independently it assumed 4-byte scales for tensors whose pack scales are fp16, and the\n    65\t    /// only reason that was not a silent wrong answer is that the re-derivation checked itself and refused.\n    66\t    /// Two places computing one layout is two places to disagree.\n    67\t    uint64_t codes_bytes = 0;\n    68\t    uint64_t scales_bytes = 0;    ///< always 4 B per scale in the arena, whatever the pack held\n    69\t    uint64_t offset_bytes = 0;    ///< 0 when the form has no offset plane\n    70\t\n    71\t    /// WHERE THIS TENSOR CAME FROM, so a caller can check the loader's work against the pack's own bytes\n    72\t    /// instead of against the loader's opinion of them.  Without `src_off` a test can only compare two\n    73\t    /// derived numbers; with it, the widening of an fp16 scale plane can be verified value by value against\n    74\t    /// `<file>.bin` - and the pack files are named, so `file_id` says which one to open.\n    75\t    uint64_t src_off = 0;\n    76\t    uint64_t src_bytes = 0;\n    77\t    int file_id = 0;\n    78\t    bool scales_fp16 = false;     ///< the PACK's width, before the loader widened it\n    79\t\n    80\t    /// WHICH ACTIVATION THIS WEIGHT WANTS - 0 for Q8_0, 1 for Q8_K.  Decided by \tools/pack_index.py from\n    81\t    /// source_type and carried here, because **the S-form CANNOT express it**: Q5_0 and Q5_K are both 8-bit\n    82\t    