{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba include/strata/kernels/cpu/expert.hpp | sed -n '1,200p'; rg -n 'struct ExpertLayout|native|blob_bytes|read_expert|load_expert|format' include/strata/kernels/cpu/expert_layout.hpp; nl -ba include/strata/prefill/prefill.hpp | sed -n '1,195p'; rg -n 'router_top|route|resid|expert|stage|cpu' src/prefill/prefill.cpp | head -85\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t// include/strata/kernels/cpu/expert.hpp - P2.S3: the CPU expert path.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// This is the Q2_0 expert kernel promoted out of `bench/micro/cpu_s2.cpp`, which is the instrument that\n     4\t// measured `c` (Memory/LEDGER.md L9/L10: 15.03 ms for one token's full routed set on 6 cores, DRAM-bound at\n     5\t// 44.14 GB/s) and whose P0.T2 parity passes at 1.461e-06.\n     6\t//\n     7\t// IT IS THE SAME CODE, NOT A COPY.  Keeping a second implementation next to a validated one is how a project\n     8\t// ends up with two answers and no way to say which is right, so `cpu_s2.cpp` is expected to become a thin\n     9\t// benchmark over these functions.  (Until it is, the two exist side by side and this header says so.)\n    10\t//\n    11\t// WHAT MAKES IT FAST, and each of the three was paid for once:\n    12\t//\n    13\t//   * `vpdpbusd` (AVX512-VNNI) does four int8 MACs per instruction.  The scalar loop it replaces ran at\n    14\t//     9.89 GB/s on one core; VNNI is the reason the 6-core figure is 42.55 GB/s.\n    15\t//   * `vpmultishiftqb` (AVX512-VBMI) unpacks the 2-bit codes eight at a time.  Its ARGUMENT ORDER is\n    16\t//     (control, data) and was determined empirically - the Intel guide states the opposite, and following the\n    17\t//     guide produced 43 of 64 wrong codes.\n    18\t//   * The row accumulator stays a FLOAT VECTOR and is reduced ONCE at the end of the row.  Reducing per\n    19\t//     64-weight block cost more than the dot products themselves: it made the kerne